    "lawCode": "Amd §§3216, 3221 & 4303, Ins L; add §2404-d, Pub Health L; amd §159-b, Civ Serv  L",
    "lawSection": "Insurance Law",
    "memo": " BILL NUMBER:  S8093\n\n TITLE OF BILL :\n\nAn act to amend the insurance law, in relation to coverage for the\ndetection of breast cancer; to amend the public health law, in\nrelation to requiring certified mammography facilities to provide\nextended hours; and to amend the civil service law, in relation to\nexcused leave to undertake a screening for breast cancer\n\n PURPOSE :\n\nIn order to facilitate early detection and treatment of breast cancer,\nthis bill would amend: (i) the Insurance Law to prohibit deductibles,\nco-payments, and coinsurance (collectively \"cost-sharing\") for\nmammograms and other screening and diagnostic imaging; (ii) the Public\nHealth Law to require certified mammography facilities to provide\nextended hours; and (iii) the Civil Service Law to allow New York City\npublic employees up to four hours of paid leave annually for breast\ncancer screenings.\n\n SUMMARY OF PROVISIONS :\n\nSections 1, 2, and 3 of the bill would amend Ins. L. § 3216 to\nprohibit cost-sharing in individual commercial policies:\n\n*Section 1 would amend Ins. L. § 3216 (i)(11)(B) to prohibit\ncost-sharing for mammography screening for breast cancer in any policy\nthat provides hospital, surgical or medical care coverage.\n\n*Section 2 would add a new subparagraph (F) to Ins. L. § 3216 (i)(11)\nto prohibit cost-sharing for screening and diagnostic imaging for the\ndetection of breast cancer, including diagnostic mammograms, breast\nultrasounds, or magnetic resonance imaging otherwise covered under the\npolicy.\n\n*Section 3 would add a new paragraph 33 to Ins. L. § 3216(i) to\nspecify that provisions requiring an insurer to provide benefits\nwithout cost-sharing apply only to: (i) participating providers in the\ninsurer's network; and (ii) in cases where the insurer does not have\nan in-network participating provider with the appropriate training and\nexperience to meet the health care needs of the insured,\nnon-participating providers.\n\nSections 4, 5, and 6 of the bill would amend Ins. L. § 3221 to\nprohibit cost-sharing in blanket or commercial policies:\n\n*Section 4 would amend Ins. L. § 3221(1)(11)(B) to prohibit\ncost-sharing for mammography screening for breast cancer in any policy\nthat provides hospital, surgical or medical care coverage.\n\n*Section 5 would add a new subparagraph (F) to Ins. L. § 3221(1)(11)\nto prohibit cost-sharing for screening and diagnostic imaging for the\ndetection of breast cancer, including diagnostic mammograms, breast\nultrasounds, or magnetic resonance imaging, covered under the policy.\n\n*Section 6 of the bill would add a new paragraph 19 to Ins. L. §\n3221(1) to specify that provisions requiring an insurer to provide\nbenefits without cost-sharing apply only to: (i) participating\nproviders in the insurer's network; and (ii) in cases where the\ninsurer does not have an in-network participating provider with the\nappropriate training and experience to meet the health care needs of\nthe insured, non-participating providers.\n\n\n\nSections 7, 8, and 9 of the bill would amend Ins. L. § 4303 to\nprohibit cost-sharing in coverage provided by health maintenance\norganizations and Article 43 corporations:\n\n*Section 7 would amend Ins. L. § 4303(p)(1)(D) to prohibit\ncost-sharing for mammography screening for breast cancer in any\ncontract that provides hospital, surgical or medical care coverage.\n\n*Section 8 would add a new paragraph 5 of Ins. L. § 4303(p) to\nprohibit cost-sharing for screening and diagnostic imaging for the\ndetection of breast cancer, including diagnostic mammograms, breast\nultrasounds, or magnetic resonance imaging, covered under the\ncontract.\n\n*Section 9 would add a new subsection (qq) to Ins. L. § 4303 to\nspecify that the provisions requiring a corporation to provide\nbenefits without cost-sharing shall only apply to: (i) participating\nproviders in the corporation's network; and (ii) in cases where the\ncorporation does not have an in-network participating provider with\nthe appropriate training and experience to meet the health care needs\nof the covered person, nonparticipating providers.\n\nSection 10 of the bill would add a new section 2404-d to the Public\nHealth L. to require any general hospital or extension clinic\ncertified as a mammography facility under the federal Mammography\nQuality Standards Act (MQSA), to provide extended hours for screening\nmammography services. Such hours must be in the early morning,\nevening, or on the weekend, at least two days each week for at least\ntwo hours each day, for a total of at least four hours each week.\nSuch hours must include the following times:\n\no Monday through Friday, between the hours of 7:00 AM and 9:00 AM;\no Monday through Friday, between the hours of 5:00 PM and 7:00 PM; or\no Saturday or Sunday, between the hours of 9:00 AM and 5:00 PM.\n\nSection 10 would also allow the Department of Health to grant a waiver\nto facilities that: (i) have insufficient staff; (ii) are in the\nprocess of discontinuing screening mammography services; or (iii) are\nsubject to hardships.\n\nSection 11 of the bill would amend Civ. Serv. L. § 159-b to repeal a\nprovision excluding public employees in cities having a population of\none million or more from receiving up to four hours of paid leave to\nget a screening for breast cancer.\n\nSection 12 would set forth the effective dates.\n\n STATEMENT IN SUPPORT :\n\nBreast cancer is the most commonly diagnosed cancer and the second\nleading cause of cancer deaths among women in New York State. Each\nyear, approximately 15,000 women are newly diagnosed with breast\ncancer, and approximately 2,640 die from the disease.\n\nScreening for breast cancer, which includes mammography, can increase\nthe detection of the disease at an early stage when treatment is most\neffective. In 2014, however, nearly 22% of women in New York aged\n50-74 reported not having mammograms at least every other year.\n\nUnder current state law, insurers that provide coverage for\nmammography screening for breast cancer must also provide coverage\nfor:\n\n* mammograms for a covered person of any age that has a prior history\nof breast cancer, or a first degree relative with a prior history of\nbreast cancer when a physician recommends the mammogram;\n* a single baseline mammogram for covered persons aged 35 to 39; and\n\n* an annual mammogram for persons aged 40 and older.\n\nHowever, individuals may still have to pay for such coverage. This\nbill would remove that financial barrier by prohibiting cost-sharing\nfor mammograms and other screening and diagnostic imaging used to\ndetect breast cancer, including diagnostic mammograms, breast\nultrasounds, and breast MRIs. If an insurer does not have a\nparticipating in-network health provider with the appropriate training\nand experience to meet the health care needs of the insured or covered\nperson, cost-sharing would also be prohibited for services provided by\nout of network providers.\n\nThis bill will also help individuals whose schedules prevent them from\ngetting screened. All certified mammography facilities must now\nprovide extended hours in the early morning, evening, or on the\nweekend for a total of at least four hours each week.\n\nFinally, to ensure equity for all public employees throughout the\nstate, this bill would eliminate a provision in the Civil Service Law\nthat excludes New York City public employees from receiving up to four\nhours of paid leave to get a screening for breast cancer.\n\n BUDGET IMPLICATIONS :\n\nNone.\n\n EFFECTIVE DATE :\nThis bill is effective immediately and shall apply to all policies and\ncontracts issued, renewed, modified, or amended on or after January 1,\n2017; provided, however, that section 11 of this bill would take\neffect on the ninetieth day after it shall become law; and provided,\nfurther, that effective immediately, the addition, amendment and/or\nrepeal of any rule or regulation necessary for the implementation of\nthis bill on its effective date is authorized and directed to be made\nand completed on or before such effective date.\n",
